  <processinfo id="aspace_84f582869dbb85e53d4ad55e04c236b3">
    <head>Processing Information</head>
<p>Processing decisions prior to 2024 are unknown. In May 2024, materials were surveyed and rehoused in archival folders and boxes. In July 2026, materials related to Doug Ashford were separated from the Group Material Archive (MSS 215) and made into a discrete collection. Materials were arranged alphabetically and described according to local and professional standards.</p><p>One CD-R was forensically imaged using Exact Audio Copy and arranged and described. New York University Libraries follow professional standards and best practices when imaging, ingesting, and processing born-digital material in order to maintain the integrity of the content.</p>  </processinfo>
  <scopecontent id="aspace_fa3ccb992d179c182f66b1b053d652aa">
    <head>Scope and Contents</head>
<p>The Doug Ashford Papers (1983-2008) contain materials related to Ashford's work as a public artist. The majority of the collection focuses on his work on the "Who Cares" Conversation series, produced with Creative Time. "Who Cares" was a series of three dinners with artists and arts professionals to discuss "their views on art's relationship to social action." The conversations led to a book published in 2006. Types of materials in the collection related to "Who Cares" include  notes and annotated transcripts. Other materials in the collection include lectures, articles, essays, and other writings by Ashford; clippings related to his works; interviews; and his notebooks.</p>  </scopecontent>
